# The South Indian Kingdoms: Pallavas, Cholas, and Their Neighbours

> From the Sangam crowns of the far south, through the Pallava
> rock-cut temples of Mamallapuram, to the imperial Cholas whose
> fleets reached Sri Lanka and Srivijaya, the Tamil country built a
> civilisation of granite, bronze, and village assemblies — the
> Dravidian temple style was born here, and local self-government
> was inscribed in stone at Uttaramerur.

## 1. The Three Crowns of the Sangam Age

Tamil poetry remembers three muventar — **Cheras**, **Cholas**, and
**Pandyas**. The Cheras ruled from Vanji (Karur) with the bow as
their emblem; the Cholas from Uraiyur and the port of **Puhar**
with the tiger; the Pandyas from Madurai with the fish. Their
bards met in the **Sangam assemblies** at Madurai, leaving the
*Ettutogai* and *Pattuppattu* collections, while **Tolkappiyam**,
the oldest Tamil grammar, fixed the classical language.

- **Karikala Chola** won the battle of Venni and raised the
  **Kallanai** (Grand Anicut) on the Kaveri — still working today.
- **Cheran Senguttuvan** is credited with the Pattini (Kannagi)
  cult; the epic **Silappadikaram** of Ilango Adigal and the
  Buddhist **Manimekalai** of Sattanar preserve the age's memory.
- Yavana (Roman) merchants settled at ports like **Muziris**; a
  Pandya embassy reached Augustus, and hoards of Roman aurei pay
  silent tribute to the pepper trade.

| Kingdom | Capital | Emblem | Famous ruler |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Chera | Vanji (Karur) | Bow | Senguttuvan |
| Chola | Uraiyur, Puhar | Tiger | Karikala |
| Pandya | Madurai | Fish | Nedunjeliyan |

After about 300 CE a shadowy **Kalabhra interregnum** covered the
Tamil country until, by the sixth century, the **Pallavas** of
Kanchipuram and the Pandyas emerged as the great rivals whose wars
and temples fill the next four centuries.

## 2. The Pallavas of Kanchipuram

**Simhavishnu** (c. 575-600) broke the Kalabhra hold and began the
imperial line. His son **Mahendravarman I** (c. 600-630) was a
scholar-king: author of the farce *Mattavilasa Prahasana*, a
convert from Jainism under the saint **Appar**, and the inventor of
rock-cut architecture — his Mandagapattu inscription boasts a
temple built "without brick, timber, metal, or mortar". The
Chalukya **Pulakeshin II** bloodied him on the northern frontier.

[important title="Vatapikonda — the conqueror of Badami"]
Mahendravarman's son **Narasimhavarman I Mamalla** (630-668)
avenged every defeat: in 642 his general Paranjothi stormed the
Chalukya capital **Vatapi (Badami)** and killed Pulakeshin II,
earning the king the title **Vatapikonda**. He also sent fleets to
help his Sri Lankan ally Manavamma, and the Chinese pilgrim
Xuanzang found Kanchi "a land of happiness" in the early 640s.
[/important]

Later Pallavas kept the style up: **Narasimhavarman II Rajasimha**
(c. 700-728) built the Shore Temple at Mamallapuram and the
Kailasanatha at Kanchi and sent an embassy of musicians to China;
**Nandivarman II Pallavamalla** (731-796) raised the Vaikuntha
Perumal temple. The dynasty's last king, Aparajita, fell to the
Chola **Aditya I** about 897, and Tondaimandalam passed to the
Cholas.

## 3. Pallava Architecture: Birth of the Dravidian Style

Pallava building moved in four recognisable steps — from caves cut
by **Mahendra**, to the free-standing monoliths of **Mamalla**, to
true granite temples of **Rajasimha**, and the perfected style of
**Nandivarman**. At **Mamallapuram (Mahabalipuram)** stand the five
chariot-shaped **rathas** (Draupadi, Arjuna, Bhima, Dharmaraja),
the open-air relief of **Arjuna's Penance** (the Descent of the
Ganga), and the Shore Temple facing the sea.

[definition]
**Vimana and gopuram** — the **vimana** is the pyramidal tower over
the sanctum, the temple's true crown, tallest at Thanjavur; the
**gopuram** is the gateway tower, later grown to record heights by
the Pandyas and Nayaks. In Pallava-Chola buildings the vimana
dominates; gopurams were still modest.
[/definition]

[note]
The Pallavas founded the **Dravidian order** of temple
architecture: granite, tiered vimanas, pillars with seated lions,
and narrative sculpture. Chola, Pandya, and Nayak builders of the
next eight centuries were simply elaborating Pallava grammar.
[/note]

## 4. The Rise of the Cholas

Around 850 **Vijayalaya** captured **Thanjavur** from the
Muttaraiyar feudatories and revived the Chola line, building the
temple of Nishumbhasudani. **Aditya I** (871-907) destroyed
Aparajita's Pallavas; **Parantaka I** (907-955) took Madurai
(title Maduraikonda) but was beaten by Rashtrakuta Krishna III at
**Takkolam (949)**. After an eclipse, **Uttama Chola** handed a
recovered kingdom to the age's two giants.

@diagram tree id="chola-line" layout="tree" aria="The Chola ruling line from Vijayalaya to Rajendra I"
  Cholas -> "Vijayalaya (c. 848-871)"
  Cholas -> "Aditya I (871-907)"
  Cholas -> "Parantaka I (907-955)"
  Cholas -> "Uttama Chola (970-985)"
  Cholas -> "Rajaraja I (985-1014)"
  Cholas -> "Rajendra I (1014-1044)"
@/diagram

## 5. Rajaraja I and Rajendra I: Empire Across the Sea

**Rajaraja I** (985-1014) burned the Chera fleet at **Kandalur
Salai**, conquered the Maldives and northern Sri Lanka, and pressed
into Karnataka (Gangapadi and Nolambapadi) and Vengi, where marriage
alliances bound the Eastern Chalukyas to his line. He conducted a
great **land survey**, reformed revenue, and between 1003 and 1010
raised the **Brihadisvara temple** at Thanjavur.

His son **Rajendra I** (1014-1044) finished the conquest of Sri
Lanka, marched an army to the **Ganga** against the Pala Mahipala I
(taking the title **Gangaikonda**), built the new capital
**Gangaikondacholapuram** with its "liquid pillar of victory", the
Cholagangam tank — and in about **1025** sent a naval expedition
across the Bay of Bengal against **Srivijaya** (Palembang and
Kadaram/Kedah), protecting the merchant guilds' sea route to
China.

@chart bar title="Reign lengths of notable Chola rulers (years)" labels:
