@/chart

Rajadhiraja I died on the field at Koppam (1054); the able
**Kulottunga I** (1070-1122), son of the Eastern Chalukya line,
abolished tolls (title *Sungam Tavirtta Cholan*, "abolisher of
tolls"), kept peace with the Chalukyas, and sent an embassy to the
Chinese Song court in 1077. Later Cholas faded before the revived
Pandyas; the last Chola king, Rajendra III, disappeared from
records by 1279.

## 6. Local Self-Government: The Uttaramerur Inscription

Chola local government is the empire's most admired institution.
Villages were of three kinds: the **ur** (peasant assembly of
ordinary villages), the **sabha** (assembly of brahmadeya villages
granted to Brahmanas), and the **nagaram** (assembly of merchants
and townspeople). The Uttaramerur inscriptions of Parantaka I
(c. 919 and 921 CE) preserve the working rules of a sabha.

[definition]
**Kudavolai system** — "pot-ticket" elections. The sabha's wards
each put forward candidates; names of qualified men were written on
palm tickets and drawn from a pot before the assembly. Committees
(*variyams*) of the chosen — for the annual accounts, gardens
(thotta), tanks (eri), gold, and fields — ran the village for a
year, with an audit before discharge.
[/definition]

| Rule at Uttaramerur | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Minimum age | 35 years |
| Property test | Owner of taxed land and a house |
| Knowledge test | Learner of the Vedas and shastras |
| Disqualifications | Tax defaulters, thieves, those who served in the past three years |
| Committees | Annual, garden, tank, gold, and field variyams |

[note]
The system was neither modern democracy nor sham: it excluded the
landless, women, and most non-Brahmanas in the sabha villages. But
its written qualifications, lottery, term limits, and audit are
world-rare for the tenth century — and the tank and garden
committees made villages self-managing economic units.
[/note]

## 7. Economy, Society, and the Temple

Land was the base: **brahmadeya** (grants to Brahmanas),
**devadana** (grants to temples), **pallichchhandam** (Jaina
grants), and **vellanvagai** (ordinary peasant villages). The land
tax (*kadamai*) took about one-third of the produce, collected by
the assembly itself in brahmadeya villages; *vetti* was compulsory
labour for public works.

- The **temple was the village bank**: it lent grain and money at
  interest, owned tanks and lands, and employed sculptors,
  musicians, accountants, dancers, and florists.
- Merchant guilds — the **Ainnurruvar** (the Five Hundred of
  Ayyavole), the **Manigramam**, and the West Asian **Anjuvannam** —
  carried textiles, pearls, and pepper from ports like
  Kaveripattinam to China and Arabia.
- Temple colleges, as at Ennayiram and Tribhuvani, boarded
  hundreds of students of the Vedas, grammar, and medicine.

## 8. Chola Art, Letters, and Decline

The Chola **bronze Nataraja** — Shiva as lord of the dance, cast by
the lost-wax process — is the period's supreme achievement, its
ageless poise described by the poet as motion within stillness. The
queen **Sembiyan Mahadevi** patronised temples and bronzes for half
a century. In letters, **Kamban** composed the *Ramavataram* in
Tamil, **Sekkilar** wrote the *Periya Puranam* of the Shaiva
saints, and Jayamkondar's *Kalingattupparani* celebrated
Kulottunga's wars.

The three **Great Living Chola Temples** — Brihadisvara at
Thanjavur (consecrated 1010), Rajendra's Gangaikondacholapuram, and
the Airavatesvara at Darasuram — are UNESCO World Heritage Sites;
all show the Chola specialisation: a towering vimana, modest
gopurams, and granite carved as if it were wood.

[tip title="Vimana first, gopuram later"]
If you see one building from a Tamil dynasty, check the tallest
tower. A colossal pyramidal tower over the sanctum means a Chola
imperial temple (Thanjavur, c. 1010); a colossal gateway tower
means later Pandyas or Nayaks, when gopurams outgrew vimanas.
[/tip]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Item | Fact |
| --- | --- |
| Sangam capitals | Vanji (Chera), Puhar (Chola), Madurai (Pandya) |
| Karikala's dam | Kallanai (Grand Anicut) on the Kaveri |
| Mahendravarman I | Mattavilasa Prahasana; first rock-cut temples |
| Narasimhavarman I | Took Vatapi, 642; title Vatapikonda |
| Chola founder of Thanjavur line | Vijayalaya, c. 850 |
| Rajendra I | Gangaikonda; Srivijaya fleet, c. 1025 |
| Uttaramerur | Kudavolai elections; committees (variyams) |
| Assemblies | ur, sabha (brahmadeya), nagaram (merchants) |
| Land tax | About one-third of produce |
| Greatest temple | Brihadisvara, Thanjavur, consecrated 1010 |
| Supreme bronze | Nataraja, lost-wax casting |
